  <!--
    Modular monolith conversion (see Agentic-AI-Platform-Microservices-Architecture.md
    Section 6 and the "modular monolith first" decision): this POM is now a pure
    aggregator/BOM - packaging=pom, no source of its own. Every package that used to
    live directly under core/src/main/java/com/agenticai/core/<pkg> now lives in its
    own Maven module below, with hard dependency boundaries between them (Maven itself
    now enforces that e.g. "providers" can never accidentally start importing
    "omnichannel" classes - a compile error, not just a code-review nit).

    Module dependency order (leaves first - a module only appears after everything it
    depends on): audit, billing, crypto, mcp, security -> admin, providers -> tenancy,
    chat -> eventbus -> approval, knowledge -> guardrails -> agents (agents+tools
    merged - see below) -> omnichannel -> app (assembles everything into the one
    deployable jar, same as before; still one process, one `mvn spring-boot:run`, now
    run from core/app).

    eventbus (added for the Interactive Workflow Designer PRD, Phase 0): depends only
    on tenancy (for TenantSchemaIterator). omnichannel depends on it directly to
    publish trigger events from TwilioWebhookController; nothing in eventbus depends
    on omnichannel, agents, or approval, so this stays a one-directional addition -
    no existing module's dependency graph changed.

    graphmemory (added after google): depends
    only on tenancy (TenantSchemaResolver - same per-tenant-schema pattern
    AgentMemorySchemaSupport already uses for pgvector, applied here to Apache AGE
    instead). agents depends on it directly (RememberFactToolCallback/GraphRecallToolCallback);
    nothing in graphmemory depends on agents, so - same shape as eventbus/devices - this
    is a one-directional addition.

    devices (Mobile SDK backend, added after omnichannel): depends only on tenancy
    (ExternalAccountProvider/TenantResolutionService/ChannelAccountLink - the same
    cross-tenant lookup omnichannel's Twilio webhook and workflow's webhook trigger
    already use, reused here rather than reinvented) and security (TenantContext).
    workflow depends on it directly for the "Device Control" node
    (WorkflowExecutionEngine#executeDeviceControlNode); nothing in devices depends
    on workflow, so - same shape as eventbus - this is a one-directional addition.

    agents+tools merged into one module, not two: AgentToolProvider (originally in
    "tools") needs the Agent entity (in "agents") to read its knowledgeBaseEnabled/
    allowed-tools config, and AgentChatService (in "agents") needs AgentToolProvider
    (in "tools") to build the toolset for the LLM call - a genuine circular
    dependency at the class level, confirmed by grepping cross-package imports
    before this split, not a guess. Maven modules cannot have circular dependencies,
    and splitting the classes apart to break the cycle would be a real code change,
    not just a file move - out of scope for "relocate, don't rewrite". Keeping them
    in one module (still called "agents") is the safe, no-behavior-change choice.

    sarvam -> providers, elevenlabs -> providers (ADR-0011 phase 2, 2026-09-29):
    until then providers depended on sarvam and elevenlabs (the kernel hosted their
    TTS/STT adapters and a hard-coded ProviderName.SARVAM credential branch). The
    arrow is now inverted: the vendor modules implement providers' SPIs
    (TextToSpeechProvider, SpeechToTextProvider, ExternalProviderCredentialSource),
    discovered by type. providers has no vendor dependency; ModuleBoundaryRulesTest
    in app enforces it.

    storage (Simple/Manual Tuning + Model Dropdown + File Upload build, Stage 3,
    added after crypto): FileStorageService, an S3-API object-storage abstraction
    (MinIO locally, swappable to AWS S3/Cloudflare R2 in prod via config only -
    see that module's pom.xml description). Leaf, no dependency on any other core
    module. sarvam depends on it directly (SarvamGeneratedAudioService, migrating
    off base64-in-Postgres); nothing in storage depends on sarvam.

    speech (SpeechTextNormalizer, added 2026-07-29 alongside storage): the one
    place raw agent/LLM text is rewritten into something a TTS engine should
    actually say out loud (markdown/HTML/emoji/control characters stripped,
    bare URLs spoken as "a link", whitespace collapsed into sentence pauses) -
    the fix for "voice reads all the special characters". A true leaf like
    crypto/storage: JDK-only static functions, zero dependency on any other
    core module. It is its own module rather than living inside one of the two
    TTS integrations because elevenlabs (ElevenLabsClient#textToSpeech) and
    sarvam (SarvamTextToSpeechClient#synthesize) both need it and neither
    depends on the other - their only shared ancestors were
    crypto/security/tenancy/storage, none of which is a sane home for text
    processing, and duplicating it into both would let the two speech surfaces
    drift apart. elevenlabs and sarvam depend on it; nothing depends on them
    from here, so this is a one-directional addition like eventbus/devices.

    sdlc (SDLC automation build plan, #849 - E2B sandbox integration scaffold,
    added after sarvam): E2bCredential BYOK storage + E2bSandboxClient (create/
    list/kill sandbox via E2B's REST API). Leaf-shaped like sarvam itself -
    depends only on crypto/security/tenancy, nothing else in core depends on
    it yet (no agent tool callback built on top of it in this scaffold pass -
    see that module's pom.xml description for the staged plan).

    voice (self-hosted in-app voice/video calling, Phase 1 of the self-reliance
    initiative, added after agents): LiveKit access-token issuance plus a
    push-to-talk STT->AgentChatService->TTS turn pipeline. Depends on agents
    (AgentService/AgentChatService), sarvam (speech-to-text leg), and
    elevenlabs (text-to-speech leg, reusing the existing voice-cloning
    integration) - a one-directional addition exactly like eventbus/
    graphmemory/devices above: nothing in agents/sarvam/elevenlabs depends on
    voice. Deliberately independent of core/omnichannel/twilio (100%
    Twilio-dependent PSTN bridge) - see voice's own pom.xml description.

    "common" (GlobalExceptionHandler, WebConfig) isn't its own module: grepping
    showed nothing depends on it, and it depends on nearly everything (the
    exception handler has an @ExceptionHandler for almost every module's custom
    exceptions) - it's cross-cutting glue, not shared low-level code, so it lives
    in "app" alongside CoreApplication and the resources (application*.yml, Flyway
    migrations).

    sharing (cross-user resource sharing foundation, added after admin): a new
    resource_grant table + ResourceAccessService for letting an Agent/Workflow
    owner grant specific teammates RUNNER/VIEWER/EDITOR access, and the
    anti-enumeration 404-on-denial contract that goes with it. Depends on
    security (TenantContext), audit (AuditLogService), and admin
    (KeycloakAdminClient, for same-tenant grantee validation - the narrower
    dependency target than pulling in tenancy, mirroring exactly what admin's
    own UserAdminController already needs it for). Deliberately has ZERO
    dependency on agents or workflow - neither Agent nor WorkflowDefinition has
    an owner column yet (that's the next workstream's job), so this module
    can't look either entity up directly; its ShareController instead resolves
    a resource's owner through a small ResourceOwnerLookup SPI (defined in this
    module, with no implementation yet) that agents/workflow will implement
    once they add the column - dependency inversion, not a forward reference.
    agents and workflow will depend ON this module in that follow-up, not the
    other way around, so listing "sharing" here right after "admin" (which it
    depends on) keeps the reactor's natural build order intact without
    reordering anything downstream.
  -->
